{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792503545476,"sessionID":"ses_ea67781bcffe0HPBy9O3ZA3rA8","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-98b954f1e7ccb565","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s1/program.md"},"output":"<path>/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s1/program.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # evoalgo: Dynamic lexicographic BFS ordering\n2: \n3: You are searching for a correct, efficient dynamic graph algorithm.\n4: You edit one file, `candidate.py`, and grade it with `evoalgo check`.\n5: \n6: ## Problem\n7: \n8: - Graph: undirected; root: no.\n9: - Implement `init(graph)`, `insert_edge(graph, edge, state)`, `delete_edge(graph, edge, state)`; each returns the new state.\n10: - `graph` is the 0/1 adjacency matrix (list of lists) after the update; `edge` is `(u, v)`. One update flips one entry.\n11: - No globals, no module-level mutable state, standard library only.\n12: \n13: State contract (what the grader reads):\n14: \n15: ```text\n16: Return a dict with 'ordering': a list of all n vertices forming a valid LexBFS\n17: ordering (any start vertex, any tie-break). Other keys are allowed and ignored.\n18: ```\n19: \n20: ## Goal for this run\n21: \n22: Reach full correctness first, then avoid a full recompute after updates.\n23: \n24: ## Loop\n25: \n26: 1. Edit `candidate.py`.\n27: 2. Run `evoalgo check` (default level `search`: all non-isomorphic graphs\n28:    up to n = 4, seconds). Use `--level examples` for a faster n <= 3 check.\n29: 3. Read the JSON verdict. On FAIL, `first_failure` names the graph, the\n30:    operation and what was wrong; fix the cause, not the symptom.\n31: 4. Keep a change if it fixes a failure or lowers `work_ratio` without\n32:    breaking correctness; otherwise revert it.\n33: 5. Every check is logged to `results.tsv` and the run folder; do not edit them.\n34: 6. When the search grade passes and you cannot lower `work_ratio` further,\n35:    run `evoalgo check --final` once (n <= 5, minutes) and report.\n36: \n37: Correct first, then cheaper. Prefer the simpler of two equally good versions.\n38: A recompute-from-scratch solution is correct but has `work_ratio` near 1;\n39: the research question is how far below that you can go.\n40: \n41: ## Research brief\n42: \n43: # Research brief: Dynamic lexicographic BFS ordering\n44: \n45: Research question: can a lexicographic BFS (LexBFS) ordering be maintained\n46: under edge insertions and deletions? No efficient fully dynamic algorithm is\n47: known - this is an open problem.\n48: \n49: The graph is undirected. The maintained result is any valid LexBFS ordering\n50: (no fixed tie-break, any start vertex). Validity is certified by the (LB)\n51: 4-point property of Corneil and Krueger: for all positions a < b < c with\n52: ac in E and ab not in E, there exists d < a with db in E and dc not in E.\n53: \n54: Known territory: the static partition-refinement LexBFS of Habib, McConnell,\n55: Paul and Viennot runs in O(n+m). Maintaining the refinement under updates is\n56: the open part; partition-refinement structures are the natural toolbox.\n57: \n58: Interface reminder: implement init(graph), insert_edge(graph, edge, state),\n59: delete_edge(graph, edge, state). The state is passed in and returned; keep no\n60: globals. Graphs are 0/1 adjacency matrices, vertex ids 0-based.\n61: \n62: This is the open tier: a correct solution would be a research contribution in\n63: its own right, so favour novel data structures over recomputation.\n\n(End of file - total 63 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# evoalgo: Dynamic lexicographic BFS ordering\n\nYou are searching for a correct, efficient dynamic graph algorithm.\nYou edit one file, `candidate.py`, and grade it with `evoalgo check`.\n\n## Problem\n\n- Graph: undirected; root: no.\n- Implement `init(graph)`, `insert_edge(graph, edge, state)`, `delete_edge(graph, edge, state)`; each returns the new state.\n- `graph` is the 0/1 adjacency matrix (list of lists) after the update; `edge` is `(u, v)`. One update flips one entry.\n- No globals, no module-level mutable state, standard library only.\n\nState contract (what the grader reads):\n\n```text\nReturn a dict with 'ordering': a list of all n vertices forming a valid LexBFS\nordering (any start vertex, any tie-break). Other keys are allowed and ignored.\n```\n\n## Goal for this run","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s1/program.md","text":"# evoalgo: Dynamic lexicographic BFS ordering\n\nYou are searching for a correct, efficient dynamic graph algorithm.\nYou edit one file, `candidate.py`, and grade it with `evoalgo check`.\n\n## Problem\n\n- Graph: undirected; root: no.\n- Implement `init(graph)`, `insert_edge(graph, edge, state)`, `delete_edge(graph, edge, state)`; each returns the new state.\n- `graph` is the 0/1 adjacency matrix (list of lists) after the update; `edge` is `(u, v)`. One update flips one entry.\n- No globals, no module-level mutable state, standard library only.\n\nState contract (what the grader reads):\n\n```text\nReturn a dict with 'ordering': a list of all n vertices forming a valid LexBFS\nordering (any start vertex, any tie-break). Other keys are allowed and ignored.\n```\n\n## Goal for this run\n\nReach full correctness first, then avoid a full recompute after updates.\n\n## Loop\n\n1. Edit `candidate.py`.\n2. Run `evoalgo check` (default level `search`: all non-isomorphic graphs\n   up to n = 4, seconds). Use `--level examples` for a faster n <= 3 check.\n3. Read the JSON verdict. On FAIL, `first_failure` names the graph, the\n   operation and what was wrong; fix the cause, not the symptom.\n4. Keep a change if it fixes a failure or lowers `work_ratio` without\n   breaking correctness; otherwise revert it.\n5. Every check is logged to `results.tsv` and the run folder; do not edit them.\n6. When the search grade passes and you cannot lower `work_ratio` further,\n   run `evoalgo check --final` once (n <= 5, minutes) and report.\n\nCorrect first, then cheaper. Prefer the simpler of two equally good versions.\nA recompute-from-scratch solution is correct but has `work_ratio` near 1;\nthe research question is how far below that you can go.\n\n## Research brief\n\n# Research brief: Dynamic lexicographic BFS ordering\n\nResearch question: can a lexicographic BFS (LexBFS) ordering be maintained\nunder edge insertions and deletions? No efficient fully dynamic algorithm is\nknown - this is an open problem.\n\nThe graph is undirected. The maintained result is any valid LexBFS ordering\n(no fixed tie-break, any start vertex). Validity is certified by the (LB)\n4-point property of Corneil and Krueger: for all positions a < b < c with\nac in E and ab not in E, there exists d < a with db in E and dc not in E.\n\nKnown territory: the static partition-refinement LexBFS of Habib, McConnell,\nPaul and Viennot runs in O(n+m). Maintaining the refinement under updates is\nthe open part; partition-refinement structures are the natural toolbox.\n\nInterface reminder: implement init(graph), insert_edge(graph, edge, state),\ndelete_edge(graph, edge, state). The state is passed in and returned; keep no\nglobals. Graphs are 0/1 adjacency matrices, vertex ids 0-based.\n\nThis is the open tier: a correct solution would be a research contribution in\nits own right, so favour novel data structures over recomputation.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":63,"totalLines":63,"truncated":false}},"title":"runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s1/program.md","time":{"start":1792503545441,"end":1792503545466}},"id":"prt_159897ccb0014Fq83sfV7moFHZ","sessionID":"ses_ea67781bcffe0HPBy9O3ZA3rA8","messageID":"msg_15989785c001DkjInk6sNLCtfT"}}
