{"type":"text","timestamp":1792505597575,"sessionID":"ses_ea67781bcffe0HPBy9O3ZA3rA8","part":{"id":"prt_159a8b58c001c5A7NW42tjNNWq","messageID":"msg_159a8ae1e001LXJwJbNOpi9evl","sessionID":"ses_ea67781bcffe0HPBy9O3ZA3rA8","type":"text","text":"\n\n## Result\n\n**Final verdict: PASS** on the full grade (all non-isomorphic graphs n≤5, every single-edge flip, plus update sequences — 21,630 cases). **work_ratio 0.368**, fitness 1.0.\n\n**Workspace:** `/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s1` (run id `eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s1`, harness `enlcode`).\n\n**The idea (two lines):** Maintain the LexBFS ordering plus adjacency bitmasks; after each single-edge flip, test whether the held ordering is still valid via the (LB) 4-point property, and only recompute a fresh ordering when it is invalidated. The key trick is that the (LB) witness set `W(b,c) = {d: b~d, ¬c~d}` equals `N(b) \\ N(c) = adj[b] & ~adj[c]` — an O(1) bitmask — so each validity test is a few bit ops.\n\n**Checks used: 8** (2 examples, 2 search to establish the baseline, 1 failed local-check attempt, 1 re-fix, 1 search to confirm, 1 final). The run ended with `harness_done`, best candidate `gen008_c01`.\n\n**What I verified:**\n- Correctness: PASS at n≤3, n≤4, and n≤5 (every case).\n- Efficiency: validate-then-recompute (0.39) beats recompute-always (0.50) — I measured both directly. A full recompute is avoided in ~72% of updates (recompute fraction 0.28).\n- One bug found and fixed: I initially compared position indices against flipped vertex ids in the local-check variant, which broke correctness; the final version uses the full (LB) check, which is both correct and cheaper.\n\n**Note:** the local O(n²) validity check I tried (rechecking only triples touching the flipped endpoints) was *more* expensive than the full check at n≤4 due to constant overhead, so I kept the full bitmask-witness check.\n\n**Cleanup:** my scratch `profile*.py` scripts are removed. Two `.nfs*` files remain in the scratch dir — they are NFS silly-rename artifacts held by a lingering sandbox subprocess and will be cleared by the kernel automatically once that process exits; I can't force-remove busy files.","time":{"start":1792505591180,"end":1792505597564}}}
